---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';
import Katex from '../../components/Katex.astro';
import '../../styles/deepDive.css';
import 'katex/dist/katex.min.css';

const title = "Post-GWAS Fine-Mapping: 95% Credible Sets, Bayesian Priors, and Epigenomic Annotations";
const description = "An advanced guide to statistical fine-mapping: Wakefield Approximate Bayes Factors, Posterior Inclusion Probabilities (PIPs), SuSiE Sum of Single Effects, PolyFun/Sniff functional priors, and eQTL colocalization.";
/* Derived from this page's own content, not guessed: prose at 200 wpm, plus 15 s per
   display equation and 3 s per inline one. These constants had drifted to ~2.5x the
   truth (this page: 761 prose words, 4 display and 28 inline equations). Part 2 moves
   the series into a content collection where this is computed, not written down. */
const readingTime = "6 min read";
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} type="article">
  <article class="deep-dive-article">
    <!-- Back to Catalog -->
    <nav style="margin-bottom: var(--space-4);">
      <a href="/deep_dives/gwas/" style="display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.35rem; font-size: var(--fs-small); color: var(--color-accent); font-weight: 600;">
        <Icon name="arrow-left" size={14} /> Back to GWAS Master Guide
      </a>
    </nav>

    <!-- Header -->
    <header class="deep-dive-header">
      <div class="deep-dive-meta-bar" style="margin-bottom: var(--space-2);">
        <span class="deep-dive-tag">GWAS Foundations · Part 8</span>
        <span class="deep-dive-level-badge"><Icon name="book-open" size={14} /> Advanced & Mathematical</span>
        <span class="deep-dive-time-badge"><Icon name="clock" size={14} /> {readingTime}</span>
      </div>
      <h1 class="deep-dive-title">{title}</h1>
      <p class="deep-dive-meta-bar">
        <span>By <strong>Kuan-Hao Chao</strong></span>
        <span>·</span>
        <span>Published August 2026</span>
      </p>
    </header>

    <!-- Lead / Introduction -->
    <p class="deep-dive-lead">
      Discovering a significant skyscraper on a Manhattan plot is only the beginning of trait mapping. Because of Linkage Disequilibrium, dozens or hundreds of candidate SNPs in an LD block exhibit identical association p-values. Furthermore, over <strong>90% of GWAS variants reside in non-coding regions</strong> (enhancers, silencers, and introns). How do we mathematically isolate the single causal nucleotide, and how does <strong>Bayesian fine-mapping (SuSiE)</strong> intersect with functional epigenomics and deep neural foundation models?
    </p>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="deep-dive-toc">
      <div class="deep-dive-toc-title">
        <Icon name="list" size={14} /> Table of Contents
      </div>
      <ol class="deep-dive-toc-list">
        <li><a href="#attribution-problem">1. The Post-GWAS Attribution Problem</a></li>
        <li><a href="#abf-math">2. Wakefield's Approximate Bayes Factor (ABF)</a></li>
        <li><a href="#pip-credible-sets">3. Posterior Inclusion Probabilities (PIPs) & 95% Credible Sets</a></li>
        <li><a href="#susie">4. SuSiE: Sum of Single Effects & Iterative Variational Inference</a></li>
        <li><a href="#functional-priors">5. Functionally Informed Priors: PolyFun & Sniff</a></li>
        <li><a href="#colocalization">6. Colocalization with eQTLs & Splicing QTLs (<Katex tex="\text{PP}_4" />)</a></li>
        <li><a href="#functional-validation">7. Molecular Epigenomics & Deep Learning Sequence Models</a></li>
        <li><a href="#summary">8. Summary & Next Steps in the GWAS Series</a></li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <!-- Chapter 1 -->
    <section id="attribution-problem">
      <h2>1. The Post-GWAS Attribution Problem</h2>
      <p>
        When a GWAS identifies a locus with <Katex tex="p = 10^{-25}" />, the reported "lead SNP" is simply the variant that happened to achieve the lowest p-value in that specific cohort.
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-callout callout-warning">
        <div class="deep-dive-callout-title">
          <Icon name="alert-triangle" size={15} /> The Two Post-GWAS Hurdles
        </div>
        <p>
          1. <strong>The Statistical Hurdle (Fine-Mapping):</strong> Disentangling which specific variant(s) in the correlated LD block are causally driving the association vs. which are merely innocent passengers.
          <br/>
          2. <strong>The Biological Hurdle (Mechanism):</strong> Mapping non-coding variants to their target effector genes, cell types, and molecular mechanisms (enhancer disruption, transcription factor binding alteration, or cryptic splicing).
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 2 -->
    <section id="abf-math">
      <h2>2. Wakefield's Approximate Bayes Factor (ABF)</h2>
      <p>
        Under a Bayesian framework, we calculate the <strong>Bayes Factor (BF)</strong> comparing the likelihood of the observed summary statistics under the hypothesis that variant <Katex tex="j" /> is causal (<Katex tex="H_1" />) versus null (<Katex tex="H_0" />). Jon Wakefield (2009) derived a closed-form analytical approximation using only the GWAS effect estimate <Katex tex="\hat{\beta}_j" /> and its variance <Katex tex="V_j = \text{SE}(\hat{\beta}_j)^2" />:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Wakefield Approximate Bayes Factor (ABF)</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\text{ABF}_j = \sqrt{\frac{V_j}{V_j + W}} \exp\left( \frac{z_j^2}{2} \cdot \frac{W}{V_j + W} \right)" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="z_j = \frac{\hat{\beta}_j}{\sqrt{V_j}}" /> = GWAS z-score &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="W" /> = Prior variance of the causal effect size (typically <Katex tex="W = 0.04 - 0.10" /> on log-odds or standardized effect scale).
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 3 -->
    <section id="pip-credible-sets">
      <h2>3. Posterior Inclusion Probabilities (PIPs) & 95% Credible Sets</h2>
      <p>
        Assuming a single causal variant in an LD block of <Katex tex="M" /> variants with prior probability <Katex tex="\pi_j" />:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Posterior Inclusion Probability (PIP)</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\text{PIP}_j = P(\gamma_j = 1 \mid \text{Data}) = \frac{\text{ABF}_j \cdot \pi_j}{\sum_{k=1}^M \text{ABF}_k \cdot \pi_k}" display={true} />
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>The 95% Credible Set (<Katex tex="\text{CS}_{95\%}" />)</h3>
      <p>
        To construct a <strong>95% Credible Set</strong>, we rank all variants in descending order of <Katex tex="\text{PIP}_j" /> and greedily add variants until their cumulative sum reaches 0.95:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">95% Credible Set Definition</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\text{CS}_{95\%} = \left\{ \text{Minimal set } \mathcal{S} \;\middle|\; \sum_{j \in \mathcal{S}} \text{PIP}_j \ge 0.95 \right\}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          Guarantees a 95% Bayesian posterior probability that the true causal variant is contained within set <Katex tex="\mathcal{S}" />. If <Katex tex="|\text{CS}_{95\%}| = 1" />, the locus is resolved to <strong>single-nucleotide causal resolution</strong>!
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 4 -->
    <section id="susie">
      <h2>4. SuSiE: Sum of Single Effects & Iterative Variational Inference</h2>
      <p>
        Many complex loci harbor multiple distinct causal variants acting independently on the same gene. Classical multi-variant fine-mapping (CAVIAR, FINEMAP) required evaluating <Katex tex="2^M" /> causal combinations, which is computationally explosive.
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-callout callout-math">
        <div class="deep-dive-callout-title">
          <Icon name="sparkles" size={15} /> The SuSiE Breakthrough (Wang et al. 2020)
        </div>
        <p>
          <strong>SuSiE (Sum of Single Effects)</strong> reformulates multiple regression as the sum of <Katex tex="L" /> single-effect vectors:
          <br/>
          <Katex tex="\mathbf{y} = \sum_{l=1}^L \mathbf{X} \mathbf{b}_l + \boldsymbol{\epsilon}, \qquad \mathbf{b}_l = \gamma_l \cdot \beta_l \quad \left( \boldsymbol{\gamma}_l \sim \text{Multinomial}(1, \boldsymbol{\pi}) \right)" display={true} />
          Each component <Katex tex="l" /> captures exactly one causal signal and its 95% Credible Set. SuSiE solves this model using the <strong>Iterative Bayesian Stepwise Selection (IBSS)</strong> algorithm, converging in linear time!
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 5 -->
    <section id="functional-priors">
      <h2>5. Functionally Informed Priors: PolyFun & Sniff</h2>
      <p>
        Standard fine-mapping assumes a uniform prior (<Katex tex="\pi_j = 1/M" />). However, a non-synonymous coding variant or an active enhancer peak is far more likely to be causal than a random intergenic base.
      </p>
      <p>
        &bull; <strong>PolyFun (Weissbrod et al. 2020):</strong> Uses Stratified LDSC to learn prior causal probabilities <Katex tex="\pi_j" /> across hundreds of genomic annotations.
        <br/>
        &bull; <strong>Sniff / Borzoi Fine-Mapping (Srivastava et al. 2025):</strong> Uses sequence-to-function predictions from the deep neural network Borzoi to evaluate single-nucleotide motif disruptions, increasing the number of fine-mapped causal variants by <strong>+9.45% across 15 UK Biobank traits</strong>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 6 -->
    <section id="colocalization">
      <h2>6. Colocalization with eQTLs & Splicing QTLs (<Katex tex="\text{PP}_4" />)</h2>
      <p>
        To determine whether a GWAS trait signal and an Expression Quantitative Trait Locus (eQTL) signal share the same underlying causal variant, we perform <strong>Colocalization analysis (coloc: Giambartolomei et al. 2014)</strong>, evaluating 5 mutually exclusive hypotheses:
      </p>

      <div class="summary-table-wrap">
        <table class="summary-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Colocalization Hypothesis</th>
              <th>Biological Meaning</th>
              <th>Actionable Conclusion</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong><Katex tex="H_0" /></strong></td>
              <td>No association with either GWAS trait or gene expression.</td>
              <td>Null locus.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong><Katex tex="H_1" /></strong></td>
              <td>Association with GWAS trait only; no eQTL.</td>
              <td>Trait mechanism does not involve this gene's steady-state expression.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong><Katex tex="H_2" /></strong></td>
              <td>Association with eQTL only; no GWAS trait association.</td>
              <td>Gene expression changes do not impact disease risk.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong><Katex tex="H_3" /></strong></td>
              <td>Both trait and gene associated, but driven by <strong>two distinct causal variants</strong> in LD.</td>
              <td>Spurious coincidence; gene is likely NOT the disease effector.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong><Katex tex="H_4" /></strong></td>
              <td>Both trait and gene associated, driven by the <strong>exact same shared causal variant</strong> (<Katex tex="\text{PP}_4 > 0.80" />).</td>
              <td><strong>Direct causal mechanism established!</strong> Gene identified as effector.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 7 -->
    <section id="functional-validation">
      <h2>7. Molecular Epigenomics & Deep Learning Sequence Models</h2>
      <p>
        Fine-mapped credible set variants are integrated with multi-modal functional layers:
        <br/>
        1. <strong>Chromatin Accessibility & Histone Marks:</strong> ENCODE ATAC-seq, H3K27ac (active enhancers), and H3K4me3 (active promoters).
        <br/>
        2. <strong>3D Chromatin Conformation:</strong> Hi-C, Micro-C, and promoter-capture Hi-C connecting distal enhancers to target promoters across megabase TAD domains.
        <br/>
        3. <strong>Experimental Screens:</strong> Massively Parallel Reporter Assays (MPRA) and pooled CRISPRi-FlowFISH screens.
        <br/>
        4. <strong>Genomic Foundation Models:</strong> Predicting in silico variant consequences on transcription initiation, alternative splicing (Splam, OpenSpliceAI), and RNA-seq coverage (Borzoi, AlphaGenome).
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 8 -->
    <section id="summary">
      <h2>8. Summary & Next Steps in the GWAS Series</h2>
      <p>
        Fine-mapping bridges statistical correlation to molecular causation, transforming raw GWAS skyscrapers into actionable single-nucleotide therapeutic targets.
      </p>

      <div class="algo-promo-card">
        <h3>Continue to Part 9: Polygenic Risk Scores (PRS) & Clinical Translation</h3>
        <p>
          Learn how genome-wide effect weights aggregate into clinical risk scores, liability threshold models, and how to address the cross-ancestry portability crisis.
        </p>
        <a href="/deep_dives/gwas-polygenic-risk-scores-prs/" class="algo-promo-btn">
          <span>Read Part 9: Polygenic Risk Scores</span>
          <Icon name="arrow-right" size={15} />
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
